# Dependencies
#
# No trailing slash on `node_modules`, and that is load-bearing rather than tidiness. A pattern
# ending in `/` matches DIRECTORIES ONLY, so a `node_modules` that is a *symlink* is not ignored,
# and `git add -A` tracks it. Two lanes in one wave did exactly that and each untracked it by hand.
# Measured here, git 2.43.0, all four cells:
#
#   pattern         `node_modules` is   git status         git check-ignore
#   node_modules/   a real directory    (ignored)          matches
#   node_modules/   a symlink           ?? node_modules    NO MATCH, exit 1
#   node_modules    a real directory    (ignored)          matches
#   node_modules    a symlink           (ignored)          matches
#
# Dropping the slash gives up nothing: the real-directory case still matches, and its contents
# still match through it — `node_modules/pkg/index.js` reports this line as its reason.
#
# The symlinks do NOT come from `.worktree-setup.sh`, which builds a real directory here; its
# header says why the whole-directory symlink fails. `DECISIONS.md` § D4 chose "symlink the root
# node_modules" and its heading still reads that way, so the practice is documented and nothing
# has withdrawn it. Whether the two tracked symlinks came from there is unestablished — the
# commits are not in this checkout. The point of the pattern is that the outcome no longer
# depends on how the directory got made.
node_modules
.pnp
.pnp.js

